<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demetrius of Phalerum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 350–c. 280 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Demetrius Phalereus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demetrius of Phalerum was an Athenian statesman and Peripatetic philosopher, a pupil of Theophrastus. Installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, he governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from 317 to 307 BCE, reforming its laws and limiting luxury, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> expelled him. He later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, where he is said to have advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> on founding the Library.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-phalerum.jpg" alt="Demetrius of Phalerum" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yair Haklai · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Demetrius_Phalereus_at_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 307 BCE his son Demetrius expelled Cassander's governor <b>Demetrius of Phalerum</b> from Athens, and in 306 BCE, after Demetrius destroyed Ptolemy's fleet off Salamis in Cyprus, Antigonus took the title of king and conferred it on his son, the first of the Successors to do so.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Demetrius of Phalerum</b> governed for Cassander from 317 to 307 BCE, and after defeat in the Chremonidean War Antigonus II Gonatas occupied the city in 262 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cassander ruled from Pella with the support of the Macedonian aristocracy, controlling much of Greece through garrisons and friendly governments, such as that of <b>Demetrius of Phalerum</b> at Athens.</blockquote><a href="/state/antipatrid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antipatrid Kingdom of Macedonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Phaleron, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.93,23.7&amp;year=-305" aria-label="Phaleron on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 800 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Demetrius of Phalerum"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1070h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1070h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.93,23.7&amp;year=-305">What was here in 305 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
